import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as o,y as r,q as s,f as d}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Appendix B Numbering Scheme and Glossary","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Appendix B Numbering Scheme and Glossary","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1524,"xkLessons":{"app-b":{"title":"Appendix B Numbering System and Terminology Table","lead":"An index helps you find definitions and conditions; it cannot satisfy them for you. Shared letters, adjacent identifiers, and counts supply no additional evidence or permission.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 4760–4804; original site location: this part’s introduction (no independently numbered anchor). See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for download and checking against the original edition.","Short quotation: “This table serves only as a navigation index.” Appendix B answers where to find definitions and conditions, rather than using a prefix to replace the full conditions. Counts are numbers of index entries, not numbers of empirically established items; adjacent identifiers do not imply inferential premises or a sequence of maturity. [T-main]","01 | D0–D3 comprise 4 items in Part Three defining structural objects, basic state terms, effective feedback, and scale transformation. Definitions fix language and the way objects are defined, but do not independently establish that real objects meet them. [T-main]","02 | U01–U11 comprise 11 items, the eleven basic structural concepts of Part Three. Basic structural concepts organize object descriptions; they are not 11 validated universal causal laws. [T-main]","03 | G1–G4 comprise 4 empirical questions in Part Four, each answered by a specific test; citing a G identifier only indicates the kind of question and cannot bypass eligibility for a test. [T-main]","04 | Laws 1–3 comprise 3 common method constraints in Part Four and Appendix C, governing respectively tasks and representations, material status and scope of support, and facts, values, and action eligibility. “Law” here is not an empirically necessary regularity or real-world legal authorization. [T-main]","05 | F1–F6 comprise 6 conditional formal propositions in Part Four and Appendix C, covering task recovery, closure under dynamics and admissible interventions, common feasible actions under information constraints, task preservation through successive conversions, error propagation under approximate closure, and the coarsest sufficient partition for a task family. Conclusions depend on their respective mathematical conditions; social cases do not automatically qualify by sharing an identifier. [T-main]","06 | E1–E5 comprise 5 epistemological constraints in Part Four. Methodological constraints can require how judgments are made, but do not themselves supply real-world effect data. [T-main]","07 | C1–C12 comprise 12 items, the twelve conclusion rules of Part Four, covering outcomes, mechanism propositions, and procedural bridges. This C prefix differs from causal-level C in the nine axes; normative bridging must be used according to its own bridging conditions. [T-main]","08 | A/X/T/O/C/R/I/N/J are the 9 axes of the scale profile in Part Five and cannot be compressed into a single micro–macro line. The scope of jurisdiction and authorization cannot be derived from the scope of the other axes; network topology scope N also differs from the five normative premises (N1–N5). [T-main]","09 | M01–M09 comprise the 9 scale transformations in Part Five. Preservation, losses, and added conditions after transformation must be checked; the name of a transformation does not establish valid transfer. [T-main]","10 | CM-* comprises the 6 conditional mechanisms in Part Six: “Effective Feedback,” “Feedback-Mediated Learning,” “Maintenance and Preservation–Dissipation,” “Load, Capacity, and Recovery,” “Phase and Threshold Transition,” and “Variation–Differential Retention–Reproduction.” The branches of each mechanism have their own conditions; the word “learning” cannot replace evidence of carriers, temporal order, or effects. [T-main]","11 | H1–H6 comprise 6 human-domain clauses in Part Seven. Check each clause’s status to determine which are empirical hypotheses, classification rules, or normative boundaries; do not call them collectively six empirical laws. [T-main]","12 | GC/GS/GE/CV/RS/IS/IM/IB comprise 8 categories in Part Seven: generative conditions/subjects/events, uptake carriers, responsible parties, and sources/mechanisms/behaviors of destabilization. The original table calls them “three sets of categories that must be kept distinct,” but this does not mean the eight labels are interchangeable; the same real entity may have different roles, yet evidence for each item is still collected separately. [T-main]","13 | HV01–HV11 comprise 11 human variables in Part Seven, described in detail in Appendix A. Listing a variable does not mean someone’s variable state has been measured, nor does it directly create authority to adjust it. [T-main]","14 | S0–S6 plus X0 are recorded as 7+1: Part Eight’s seven human state prototypes plus one orderly exit path. 7+1 is not eight stages, X0 is not called S7, and numbering does not rank value. [T-main]","15 | IG1–IG7 comprise 7 gates and are Part Thirteen’s only main workflow; 16 | SJ1–SJ8 comprise 8 strong-judgment materials; 17 | L1–L3 comprise 3 diagnostic levels. Workflow, strong-judgment components, and levels of effort are different dimensions, not one continuous process. [T-main]","18 | TOOL-* comprises 6 formal tools in Part Thirteen: Source–Evidence Ledger, Open Assertion Record, Proposition Validation Form, Forecast Registration, Counterexample and Scale Stress Test, and AI Output Boundaries. Tool records can carry checks, but complete forms do not self-validate a judgment. [T-main]","19 | N1–N5 comprise the 5 normative premises in Part Fourteen, with each normative choice stating which of them it actually adopts; 20 | O1–O4 comprise the 4 steps of the operational procedure for bounded authorization; 21 | PF-1–PF-10 comprise the 10 items of the protection floor. Value choices, authorization procedures, and the protection floor are separate; normative N differs from topological N, and procedural O differs from organizational-scale O. [T-main]","22 | T0–T4 comprise 5 intervention levels in Part Fifteen; 23 | APP-* comprises 9 application modules: “Relationships,” “Families,” “Organizations and Projects,” “Platforms,” “Institutions and Public Governance,” “Public Opinion,” “Space and Channels,” “Emergencies and High-Pressure Situations,” and “Ultra-Large Scale.” Intervention levels are not empirical confidence levels, and application names do not grant authority in the corresponding domain. [T-main]","24 | GOV-01–GOV-24 comprise 24 governance clauses in Part Sixteen. Governance constraints and application modules cross-reference each other, but the index does not replace each clause’s applicability conditions and action boundaries. [T-main]","Recurring notation must also be read item by item: S*=<B,X,R,I,T,SP,K> denotes the seven elements of the definition of a structural object—in order, boundary, state variables, relations or transition constraints, interface, time window, scale profile, and identity criterion. These local letters cannot be interpreted directly as the same letters among the nine axes of the scale profile; R in S* is not observation resolution (R), I is not scope of influence (I), and X is not spatial extent (X). [T-main]","N0 denotes the null model, not a zeroth item preceding the five normative premises (N1–N5); K* and F* denote the target identity criterion and the target functional criterion for repair, respectively. G-instance and H-instance refer to a specific test of an empirical question and of a human-domain clause, respectively; having an identifier does not mean having received support. F1–F6 are the identifiers of the conditional formal propositions; standalone F/F* may mean dynamic update, functional criterion, or repair target under local definitions. The same letter does not imply the same object. [T-main]","The final historical boundary is a substantive reminder: the historical increment tests only a historical conditional increment beyond a given current representation. Carrier-mediated learning, accumulation, persistence, and intertemporal reproduction are checked under the conditions of their respective mechanisms; positive support from a historical-increment test is not their shared prerequisite. When consulting the index, do not attach a historical-increment test to all these terms merely because they involve time. [T-main]","In practice, first identify the current sentence’s namespace, local definition, object, and version; then return to the definitions and conditions in the main text; finally check test materials and the conclusions that can be drawn. This appendix makes no external empirical claims; forcing in a historical case would confuse “navigational use” with “theory validation,” so this topic uses explicitly constructed naming-collision exercises without claiming they are historical cases. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Identify the prefix and local namespace, then consult the corresponding main-text part.","Separate the number of identifiers from the number of items supported by tests.","Distinguish definitional, formal, empirical, normative, procedural, and tool status.","Check local definitions each time for shared letters in S* and SP, F/F*, and N0/N1–N5.","A historical-increment test applies only to increments beyond the current representation; check other intertemporal conditions separately.","Cite main-text conditions and test materials; do not substitute the index for evidence."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"S* object definition","leftText":"Seven elements organize a candidate object.","rightTitle":"SP scale profile","rightText":"Nine axes of scope and resolution, embedded in the object's definition."},{"leftTitle":"F1—F6","leftText":"The identifiers of the six conditional formal propositions.","rightTitle":"F or F*","rightText":"Local notation for dynamics or function/target."},{"leftTitle":"N topology axis","leftText":"Network scope.","rightTitle":"N1—N5","rightText":"The five normative premises; N0 is separately a null model."},{"leftTitle":"Seven prototypes plus one path","leftText":"The seven state prototypes and “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise.”","rightTitle":"Eight stages","rightText":"The index provides no such evolutionary sequence."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample: interpreting X in S* as SP’s spatial axis reads the wrong item.","Teaching counterexample: calling “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise” the eighth stage of maturity violates the 7+1 type distinction.","Boundary in the original: the appearance of learning or intertemporal terms does not justify requiring positive support from a historical-increment test in every case.","Boundary in the original: the navigation table does not replace main-text definitions, conditions, and use restrictions."],"questions":["Can the table’s counts be treated as numbers of validated theories?","Must F* be one of F1–F6?","Is a historical-increment test a prerequisite for all intertemporal learning?","How can misreading of shared letters be avoided?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/APP-B/figures/index-lookup.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"app-b","start_line":4817,"end_line":4861,"startLine":4817,"endLine":4861}],"outputId":"app-b","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: shared letters still need local definitions","text":"A constructed record states S*.R=relations among roles and SP.R=weekly aggregation resolution. These are not contradictory assignments to the same R, but different items in the object's definition and the scale profile. Putting the former into the latter would lose both relations and compression losses.","assumptions":"Both records and assignments are fictional, illustrating namespaces only.","boundary":"No real object identification or causal conclusion is made."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the arithmetic of 7+1 cannot become a stage theory","text":"The index contains 7 state prototypes and 1 path of “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise”; its entry count may be calculated as 8, but the types remain 7+1. Adding counts does not produce an eighth state, nor a condition requiring completion of all 7 prototypes before exit.","assumptions":"Only a set-counting explanation; names come from the user’s original text.","boundary":"No extrapolation to laws of organizational evolution or authorization to exit."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: interpreting X in S* as SP’s spatial axis reads the wrong item.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: calling “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise” the eighth stage of maturity violates the 7+1 type distinction.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: the appearance of learning or intertemporal terms does not justify requiring positive support from a historical-increment test in every case.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: the navigation table does not replace main-text definitions, conditions, and use restrictions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can the table’s counts be treated as numbers of validated theories?","answer":"No. They are counts of index entries; evidence status is checked separately.","basis":"Navigation boundary."},{"question":"Must F* be one of F1–F6?","answer":"Not necessarily; local definitions decide, and letters alone cannot establish correspondence.","basis":"Recurring notation."},{"question":"Is a historical-increment test a prerequisite for all intertemporal learning?","answer":"No. It is limited to historical increment beyond the current representation; other routes follow their respective conditions.","basis":"Appendix final paragraph."},{"question":"How can misreading of shared letters be avoided?","answer":"First mark namespaces such as object definition/scale/normative/formal proposition, then read local definitions.","basis":"Same names do not mean the same things."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/APP-B/figures/index-lookup.png","alt":"Use prefixes to find definitions and conditions in the main text, then check test evidence and ceilings; counts do not equal support.","caption":"Based on Appendix B’s navigational role."},{"path":"/reading-assets/APP-B/figures/index-names.png","alt":"Separate S* from SP, F1–F6 from F/F*, N0 from normative/network N, and S from X0.","caption":"Based on Appendix B’s final paragraphs and numbering table."}],"diagramBrief":"The two figures show the index-use path and major naming collisions; all 24 rows and recurring notation are explained item by item in the text.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section navigates the numbering and notation internal to the user’s theory; it proposes no external historical facts or empirical effects. Explicit teaching exercises on naming collisions are used, without inventing history to fill a case quota."},"openQuestions":["This index cannot replace source checks for the definitions and conditions in the main text or for real-world cases."],"taskId":"APP-B","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§app-b, Chinese source lines 4318–4355","claim_support":["All 24 index rows, recurring notation, and the G3 boundary."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"附录B　编号体系与术语总表; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/APP-B/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/APP-B/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/APP-B/source/APP-B.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/APP-B/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"896a589953ba447198ee3486b62df71b3389364fdb2e1607e7fdb9193e42683c","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-b.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-b.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-b.md"};function l(p,e,h,u,m,f){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><p><a id="part_18"></a></p><h1 id="part-title-18" tabindex="-1">Appendix B Numbering Scheme and Glossary <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-title-18" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Appendix B Numbering Scheme and Glossary {#part-title-18}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The book uses the numbering schemes below. The running text refers to every item by its name; identifiers appear only at the end of the heading of the section where each item is defined. This table serves only as a navigation index. The relevant parts of the main text govern each scheme’s definitions, conditions, and limits on use.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Identifier</th><th>Name</th><th>Count</th><th>Where Defined</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D, G, E, H, N, O, F</td><td>The seven proposition roles: definitions; empirical questions; epistemic constraints; human-domain clauses; normative commitments; operational procedures; conditional formal propositions</td><td>7</td><td>Part 2 (§2.2)</td></tr><tr><td>EVIDENCE, CAUSAL, ANALOGY, SOURCE</td><td>The four method conditions: conditions for evidence; conditions for causal claims; conditions for analogy; conditions for sources</td><td>4</td><td>Part 2 (§2.4–§2.7)</td></tr><tr><td>D0–D3</td><td>The definition layer: structural object; basic state terms; effective feedback; scale transformation</td><td>4</td><td>Part 3 (§3.1–§3.4)</td></tr><tr><td>U01–U11</td><td>The eleven basic structural concepts: Candidate Object; Boundaries and Interfaces; States and Observables; Components, Carriers, and Substrates; Relations and Constraints; Flow, Channels, and Transmission; Feedback and State Updating; Conditions and Environment; Load, Capacity, and Recovery Margin; Trace, Memory, and Paths; Phase, Transition, and Dissolution</td><td>11</td><td>Part 3 (§3.5)</td></tr><tr><td>G1–G4</td><td>The four empirical questions: grouping gain (conditional gain from candidate grouping); channel effect (specified-channel effect); historical increment (historical conditional increment beyond the current representation); scale closure (representational closure and object or intervention conversion). Each is answered by a specific test, called a grouping-gain test, a channel-effect test, a historical-increment test, and a scale-closure test, respectively</td><td>4</td><td>Part 4 (§4.3–§4.6)</td></tr><tr><td>G1a–G4b</td><td>The two subtypes of each empirical question: for grouping gain, the predictive-gain subtype and the intervention-gain subtype; for channel effect, the controlled-identification subtype and the natural-variation subtype; for historical increment, predictive path dependence and causal path dependence; for scale closure, the closure-failure subtype and the subtype for object or intervention conversion</td><td>8</td><td>Part 4</td></tr><tr><td>CMC1–CMC3</td><td>The three common method constraints: Tasks and Representations Must Be Declared Together; The Status of Materials and Scope of Support Must Be Traceable; Facts, Values, and Eligibility to Act Are Established Separately</td><td>3</td><td>Part 4 (§4.17) and Appendix C</td></tr><tr><td>F1–F6</td><td>The conditional formal propositions: Task Recovery; Closure under Dynamics and Admissible Interventions; Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints; Task Preservation through Successive Conversions; Error Propagation under Approximate Closure; The Coarsest Sufficient Partition for a Task Family</td><td>6</td><td>Part 4 (§4.18–§4.19) and Appendix C</td></tr><tr><td>E1–E5</td><td>The five epistemic constraints: Constraint on Object Declaration; Observation Position and Model Limits; Conditional Observational Participation; Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals; Testing Cross-Scale Transfer</td><td>5</td><td>Part 4 (§4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>C1–C12</td><td>The twelve conclusion rules, covering outcomes, propositions about mechanisms, and procedural bridges: Grouping Gain and Its Robustness to Boundary Perturbation; Carrying and Channel Constraints; Effective Feedback and Feedback-Mediated Learning; Historical Effects, Path Differences, and Repair Windows; Load, Capacity, and Recovery; Scale Closure and Object Conversion; Local Improvement and Cross-Boundary Spillover; Variation–Differential Retention–Reproduction; Observation Channels and Reflexive Responses; Human Uptake and Intertemporal Reproduction; Institutional Write-Back; Normative Bridging</td><td>12</td><td>Part 4 (§4.10–§4.14)</td></tr><tr><td>A, X, T, O, C, R, I, N, J</td><td>The nine axes of the scale profile: aggregation level; spatial extent; time span; organizational level; causal level; observation resolution; scope of influence; network topology scope; scope of jurisdiction and authorization</td><td>9</td><td>Part 5 (§5.2)</td></tr><tr><td>M01–M09</td><td>The nine scale transformations: Aggregation; Nesting; Network Propagation; Accumulation Over Time; Institutionalization; Emergence; Delegation or Representation; Compression or Abstraction; Horizontal Transfer</td><td>9</td><td>Part 5 (§5.5)</td></tr><tr><td>CM-*</td><td>The six conditional mechanisms: Effective Feedback (FEEDBACK); Feedback-Mediated Learning (LEARNING); Maintenance and Preservation–Dissipation (MAINTENANCE); Load, Capacity, and Recovery (LOAD); Phase and Threshold Transition (PHASE); Variation–Differential Retention–Reproduction (SELECTION)</td><td>6</td><td>Part 6 (§6.2–§6.8)</td></tr><tr><td>H1–H6</td><td>The six human-domain clauses: Meaning and Coordination; Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility; Institutional Write-Back; Power, Mediation, and Reflexivity; Historical Trace Carriers; Open-Ended Undertaking</td><td>6</td><td>Part 7 (§7.1)</td></tr><tr><td>GC, GS, GE, CV, RS, IS, IM, IB</td><td>The three sets of categories that must be kept distinct: generative condition, generative subject, and generative event; uptake carrier and responsible party; source of destabilization, mechanism of destabilization, and behavior of destabilization</td><td>8</td><td>Part 7 (§7.2)</td></tr><tr><td>HV01–HV11</td><td>The eleven human variables: Structural Domain; Boundaries and Interfaces; Orientation Anchor; Generative Node; Action Uptake Layer; Drive–Uptake Chain; Feedback Write-Back; Conditional Potential Field; Structural Load; Evolutionary Phase; Open-Ended Undertaking Action (described in detail in Appendix A)</td><td>11</td><td>Part 7 (§7.3)</td></tr><tr><td>S0–S6, X0</td><td>The seven human state prototypes: Prototype of Coalescence Around an Anchor; Prototype of a Generative Core Taking Shape; Prototype of Initial Expansion and Differentiated Uptake; Prototype of Development Under Interconnected Constraints; Prototype of a Window for Structural Reconfiguration; Prototype of Efficient Coordination and Flexible Expansion; Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance. There is also the path of Orderly Exit and Benign Demise</td><td>7+1</td><td>Part 8 (§8.3, §8.5)</td></tr><tr><td>IT1–IT4</td><td>The four conversions: structural variables to observable signals; signals to strength of evidence; strength of evidence to level of judgment; level of judgment to requirements and ceiling for action</td><td>4</td><td>Part 13 (§13.1)</td></tr><tr><td>IG1–IG7</td><td>The seven gates, the only main workflow: Effective Object Gate; Evidence Traceability Gate; Affected Positions Gate; Power and Anti-Retaliation Gate; Mediation and Reflexivity Gate; Strong-Judgment Procedure Gate; Action Ceiling Gate</td><td>7</td><td>Part 13 (§13.3)</td></tr><tr><td>FG1–FG5, DS01–DS13</td><td>The five gates: Object Gate; Evidence Gate; Scale Gate; Responsibility Gate; Observation Gate. There are also thirteen steps; the name of each step is given in the main text</td><td>5, 13</td><td>Part 13 (§13.4)</td></tr><tr><td>SJ1–SJ8</td><td>The strong-judgment kit: Contrary conditions; Repair window; Evidence requirements; Access to appeal; Anti-retaliation protection; Right to add evidence; Trigger for external review; Restoration and write-back</td><td>8</td><td>Part 13 (§13.5)</td></tr><tr><td>L1–L3</td><td>The three diagnostic levels: lightweight description; standard judgment; strong-judgment candidate</td><td>3</td><td>Part 13</td></tr><tr><td>TOOL-*</td><td>The six formal tools: Source–Evidence Ledger (EVIDENCE-LEDGER); Open Assertion Record (OPEN-ASSERTION); Proposition Validation Form (CLAIM-VALIDATION); Forecast Registration (FORECAST-REGISTRY); Counterexample and Scale Stress Test (STRESS-TEST); AI Output Boundaries (AI-BOUNDARY)</td><td>6</td><td>Part 13 (§13.7)</td></tr><tr><td>DF1–DF9</td><td>The nine gates for multi-circle projection: Facts as They Stand; Joint Object; Two Channels and Clocks; Propagation Through Mechanisms; Branching Paths; Forecast Registration; Normative Choice; Authorization and Execution; Outcome Write-Back</td><td>9</td><td>Part 13 (§13.10)</td></tr><tr><td>SEL-SYS, SEL-AGT, SEL-GOV</td><td>The three processes of selection and choice: systemic selection; decision-making by a subject; collective governance</td><td>3</td><td>Part 14 (§14.2)</td></tr><tr><td>N1–N5</td><td>The five normative premises, with each normative choice stating which of them it actually adopts: Explanation Does Not Confer Authority to Act; Protection Must Not Be Downgraded; High-Impact Judgments Must Be Correctable; Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted; Persistence and Systemic Selection Do Not Establish Normative Justification</td><td>5</td><td>Part 14 (§14.3)</td></tr><tr><td>NSP-LEAST-HARM, NSP-PROPORTIONALITY</td><td>The principle of least harm; the principle of proportionality</td><td>2</td><td>Part 14 (§14.4)</td></tr><tr><td>O1–O4</td><td>The four-step operational procedure for bounded authorization: Fixing the Facts and Evidence; Stating the Normative Premises and Conflicts; Reviewing Authorization and Comparing Options; Bounded Execution and Correction</td><td>4</td><td>Part 14 (§14.6)</td></tr><tr><td>PF-1–PF-10</td><td>The ten items of the protection floor: Listing Affected Positions and Low-Power Positions; Low-Exposure Channels for Submission; Genuinely Effective Refusal and Stopping; Effective Appeal; Anti-Retaliation; Adding Evidence and Correcting Records; External Independent Review; Actual Capability for Withdrawal, Restoration, and Repair; Genuine Exit and Alternative Uptake; Action Ceiling and Expiry</td><td>10</td><td>Part 14 (§14.8)</td></tr><tr><td>T0–T4</td><td>The five intervention levels: Carriers of Safety and Trust; Reversible Minimum Order; Repair of the Uptake–Return Flow Chain; Deep-Time Maintenance and Higher-Order Updating; Exit, Transfer, and Preservation of Evolutionary Memory</td><td>5</td><td>Part 15 (§15.2)</td></tr><tr><td>APP-*</td><td>The nine application modules: Relationships (REL); Families (FAM); Organizations and Projects (ORG); Platforms (PLT); Institutions and Public Governance (INS); Public Opinion (OPI); Space and Channels (SPC); Emergencies and High-Pressure Situations (EMG); Ultra-Large Scale (ULS)</td><td>9</td><td>Part 15 (§15.3)</td></tr><tr><td>GOV-01–GOV-24</td><td>The twenty-four governance clauses: Framework Self-Diagnosis and Separation of Objects; Consensus Procedure and Authority Over Versions; Correcting Empirical Assertions and Choosing Which of the Four Empirical Questions to Keep; Tracking Cases, Counterexamples, Assertions, and Forecasts; The Pre-Concept Gate and Ceiling on Conceptual Validity; Absence Signals, Protective Opacity, and Suppressive Opacity; Operating Limits of the Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance and Stopping Observational Recursion; Malicious Compliance and Checking for Selective Evidence; AI Boundaries and What Process Outputs Can Establish; Six Components of Weak-Signal Protection; An Intermediate Path Without Institutional Infrastructure; Protection of Subjects Unable to Exit; Complex Trauma and the Absence of a Healthy Baseline; Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate; Knowledge Lineage and Source Transparency; Usage-Threshold Debt and Accessibility Review; Tool Use, Commercialization, Weaponization, and Risks of Forking; Retiring Open Assertions Captured by Power; Version Write-Back and the Feedback Loop; External Review Packages and Checks Before Public Release; Benign Demise of the Framework and Transition to a Replacement; Multi-Circle Complexity and Governance of Foresight; Governing Personality, Privacy, and Overreach by Automation; Model Degradation, Retirement, and Replacement</td><td>24</td><td>Part 16 (§16.1–§16.24)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Several other notations recur throughout the book. S*=&lt;B,X,R,I,T,SP,K&gt; denotes the seven elements of the definition of a structural object: in order, boundary, state variables, relations or transition constraints, interface, time window, scale profile, and identity criterion. N0 denotes the null model. K* and F* denote the target identity criterion and the target functional criterion for repair, respectively. G-instance and H-instance refer to a specific test of an empirical question and of a human-domain clause, respectively. The first definition of each in the main text governs its use.</p><p>F1–F6 are the identifiers of the conditional formal propositions. Used on their own, F and F* follow their local definitions and can denote a dynamic update, a functional criterion, or a repair target; use of the same letter does not establish that the object is the same. The historical increment (G3) tests only a historical conditional increment beyond the given current representation. Learning, accumulation, sustained effects, and intertemporal reproduction mediated by carriers are tested under the conditions of their respective mechanisms; positive support from a historical-increment test is not a common prerequisite for them.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"app-b"}),e[1]||(e[1]=d("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=i(c,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,b as default};
